    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d474">

                <pb facs="0473" n="463" sameAs="frus:frus1943v01/pg463"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 European War 1939/1041: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-08T22:00:00+02:00">August 8, 1943—10
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1387. In conversation with the British Ambassador<anchor n="11" xml:id="d474-f1"
                    /> he said that the Foreign Minister had rejected the suggestion of certain
                    other neutrals for joint action their reply to the American, British and <pb
                        facs="0474" n="464" xml:id="pg464"/> Soviet notes concerning asylum to war
                    criminals and had advised the Swedish, Swiss and Portuguese Ministers to make
                    “friendly” replies. Hugessen said Numan expressed the opinion that it was
                    “unfortunate” the British note had not followed the form of the American note
                    which was much easier to deal with as it contained no implied threat. Numan
                    promised to discuss with Hugessen the form and substance of the Turkish reply
                    before reaching a final decision, adding that the publicity given the notes had
                    made his position even more difficult. Toward the close of their talk Numan
                    remarked to Hugessen that in any event the Turkish Government would refuse
                    asylum to Mussolini and his henchmen.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
